[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it held three
problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: one problem fixed, one gone with its form, one
not addressed.

<Callout kind="warn">
Closed, not all fixed. The one-day moves had no fix. If they come back, reopen their ticket.
</Callout>

## What was in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## What happened to each

- **`parser` · fixed.** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` shipped with the close of #4471.
- **`hint` · won't fix.** The `booking-v3` form is retired, and its `MM/DD/YYYY` hint with it.
- **`one-day` · not addressed.** Neither change touches time zones. Ticket closed with #4471;
  reopen it on the next one-day report.

## How each report was placed

1. **One report per id.** The export pages overlap, so some reports come twice. Count each id once.
2. **Where it comes from, not what intake guessed.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser.
   The cause depends on the tenant: their locale and their booking form. A tenant in Lisbon with an
   American browser is still a day-first tenant.
3. **`hint`:** the report came through the booking form of a day-first tenant on `booking-v3`. That
   form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date, so people typed month first and the form read it day first.
4. **`parser`:** every other report from a day-first tenant.
5. **`one-day`:** the desk read these as a one-day move (desk notes): Tuesday became Monday, an invite
   came before its booking, one meeting held twice by clients in two time zones. A flipped month
   cannot do that. They stay counted where they are filed, and have their own ticket.

## Next time

Merging by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put all three
problems in one ticket. The same subject line does not mean the same cause.

